  "story_title": "There’s a Hole in the Bottom of the C: Effectiveness of Allocation Protection [pdf]",
  "parent_comment": "I still think that the Deputy's project (from UC Berkeley) approach (https://barnowl.org/research/pubs/07-hotos-linux.pdf) is the most practical for adding bounds checks to existing C code. Basically annotate functions and data structures with the, usually available, bounds information, e.g.:

  f(int *a, int n) -> f(int *count(n) a, int n);

or

  struct { int len; int *values; }
->

  struct { int len; int *count(len) values; }

And other annotations for unions, etc.

This actually works quite well in practice - the cited paper involved us applying these annotations to a complete, bootable l",
  "comment": "Depends on if one would rather write:

    int s[3];
    int *p;
    f(s, sizeof(s)/sizeof(s[0]));
    f(p, n);

    f(int *count(n) a, int n);

(which has a mistake, n should be size_t not int), or:

    int s[3];
    int* p;
    int[] a;
    f(p[0..n]);   // convert pointer to phat pointer
    f(a);         // or use existing phat pointer
    f(s);         // static arrays just work

    f(int[] a);   // the declaration of f()",
  "posted_year": 2018
}

